course: dictionary
lesson: 764
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word beagle can name a kind of dog, and it can also name a famous ship. You can tell which one is meant by reading the words around it, which is the context.
explain: «At the airport a beagle sniffs a line of suitcases.» The words airport and suitcases tell you this is the dog, because a ship does not sniff luggage. Ask: which meaning fits the sentence? Then swap your meaning in and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «Charles Darwin sailed round the world on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836.» Here Beagle is a name, with capital letters and HMS in front of it, so it means the ship. Capital letters and words like HMS are clues too.
fact: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant.
fact: A beagle is a small to medium hunting dog with long floppy ears, short legs and a white, brown and black coat.
fact: «Beagle» can also be a name: HMS Beagle was the ship Charles Darwin sailed on to study nature.
fact: Beagles have a great sense of smell, so they are trained to sniff out things at airports, such as food in luggage.
fact: Capital letters often show a name, as in HMS Beagle.
exercise: «At the airport a beagle sniffs a line of suitcases.» What does beagle mean here? | A large airport building | A kind of suitcase | HMS Beagle, the ship Charles Darwin sailed on | *A small hunting dog with long floppy ears
exercise: «In 1835 Charles Darwin visited on the ship Beagle.» What does Beagle mean in this sentence? | A kind of bird | *The ship HMS Beagle | A tortoise on an island | A small hunting dog
exercise: Asha says the beagle in «sailed round the world on HMS Beagle» must be a dog. Who is right? | *Asha is wrong, because Beagle is a ship here | Nobody can tell, because there are no clues | Asha is right, because it is a dog | Both are right at the same time
test: «Darwin visited the Galápagos on the Beagle in 1835.» Is Beagle a dog or a ship here? | Neither | A dog | Both at once | *A ship
test: Which clue shows that a beagle is the dog in «a beagle sniffs a line of suitcases and suddenly sits down»? | The capital letters in HMS Beagle | *Sniffs and sits, which is what a hunting dog does | The name Charles Darwin | The year 1835
exam: Tom reads «The Beagle carried Darwin around the world» and says it is a dog. How could he check? | Guess, because the word looks like a dog | Stop reading and skip the whole text | Ignore the sentence and only read the first word | *Use the clues: a ship carries people around the world, and the capital B and Darwin show a name
